<Layout title="The House">
 <section class="page-opening wrap"><p class="eyebrow">AN OPEN SKY AT THE CENTRE</p><h1>Step inside.<br/><em>Stay a little.</em></h1><p class="opening-description">A house held together by its courtyard. From the shaded passage to the rooftop, every space has its own light and its own rhythm.</p></section>
 <section class="wrap house-hero" id="courtyard"><Photo id={1} eager sizes="100vw"/><div class="photo-caption"><span>01 / THE COURTYARD</span><span>The heart of the house.</span></div></section>
 <section class="wrap section editorial-pair"><figure><Photo id={2}/><figcaption>The threshold between street and house.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">FROM STREET TO STILLNESS</p><h2>A turn in the passage.<br/><em>A change of pace.</em></h2><p>The entrance bends before it opens. Beyond the timber door, daylight falls into the courtyard and water gathers in a small green-tiled fountain. The rooms look inward, toward this shared centre.</p><p>DAR SERA imagines a contemporary domestic setting in Tetouan, with a restrained palette of lime, timber, stone and linen.</p></div></section>
 <div id="house-map"><Explorer/></div>
 <section class="wrap section editorial-wide" id="lounge"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE SHARED SPACES</p><h2>A place to read.<br/><em>A table to gather.</em></h2></div><p>The lounge and breakfast room sit on opposite sides of the courtyard. Leave your door, and find a different corner.</p></div><Photo id={3} sizes="100vw"/><div class="house-secondary"><div><h3>The lounge</h3><p>Linen seating, timber chairs and room for an unplanned hour. The courtyard light moves across the floor throughout the day.</p></div><figure><Photo id={4}/><figcaption>The breakfast room · a shared table beside the courtyard.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
 <section class="material-section section"><div class="wrap editorial-pair"><div><p class="eyebrow">MATERIAL, LIGHT, TEXTURE</p><h2>Simple things.<br/><em>Closely considered.</em></h2><p>The softness of lime plaster. The grain of dark timber. Green ceramic at the water’s edge. Materials give the house its continuity, from the courtyard to each private room.</p><p>The upper gallery links the bedrooms above. Stairs connect the ground floor, gallery and rooftop; there is no lift in this concept.</p></div><div class="material-images"><Photo id={25}/><Photo id={6}/></div></div></section>
 <section class="wrap section rooftop-story" id="rooftop"><p class="eyebrow">ABOVE THE HOUSE</p><h2>Let the afternoon<br/><em>take its time.</em></h2><Photo id={5} sizes="100vw"/><div class="rooftop-caption"><p>A shared rooftop with timber shade, linen cushions and a low roofscape beyond. Separate from the Terrace Suite’s private terrace, and reached by stairs.</p><a class="text-link" href="/rooms/">Find your room <span>↗</span></a></div></section>
</Layout>
